  "image_name": "aa5e3f0b1ce4999bbb8493aaa001eb8f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares major hydrogen sources, how they are produced, and the common color labels used to classify them, along with approximate carbon footprints and costs. It shows four pathways: coal, natural gas, renewables, and naturally occurring hydrogen.\n\nCoal and natural gas are linked to fossil-fuel production routes. Coal leads to “black” or “grey” hydrogen, while natural gas is used in steam methane reformation; when this route includes carbon capture and storage, it becomes “blue” hydrogen. These fossil-based options have the highest emissions overall, with black/grey shown around 10–25 kg CO2 per kg H2 and blue lower at roughly 1–10 kg CO2 per kg H2. Their costs appear relatively low to moderate.\n\nRenewables produce “green” hydrogen through electrolysis of water powered by wind and solar electricity. This route has by far the lowest listed emissions, about 0.3 kg CO2 per kg H2, but the highest current cost, with a note suggesting lower future costs by 2050.\n\nA separate pathway shows “natural” hydrogen coming directly from geological sources or water–rock/radiolysis processes. It appears to have a low carbon footprint and potentially competitive cost, though some exact details are less emphasized than for the other routes.",
